torname{ln}⁡(0.99) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548994 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548994 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548994 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $-(0.33)^{4x+1}\\le (0.12)^{x-4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36396874-2543-5347-91a0-563733d2b600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548994 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $(0.12)^{x-4}$ is always positive and $-(0.33)^{4x+1}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n-(0.33)^{4x+1}<(0.12)^{x-4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549001 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality ${(0.45)}^{x+3}-10\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46e111c4-e425-556e-9ed0-200d0e8bd4c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549001 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.45)}^{x+3}-10 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {(0.45)}^{x+3} & \\ge 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.45)^{x+3}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ (x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549014 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549014 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{3x-1}+1>0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74b59c8c-c1aa-50c4-b3ef-52e18b9395b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549014 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{3x-1}+1 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{3x-1} & >-1 \\\\ {7}^{3x-1} & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $7>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{7}^{3x-1}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${7}^{3x-1}$ is greater than $-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{7}^{3x-1}>-2\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549042 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $4{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1}-8\\le 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1b6f8d1-34e2-5323-8ed2-7acebb0e0e39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549042 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1}-8 & \\le 0 \\\\ 4{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1} & \\le 8 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1} & \\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{7}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ (x-1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549049 #edb/ref 1069 "q-277850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549049 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549049 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2}}{2\\sqrt{e}}>e.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549049 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c14a17a4-e51c-5a30-87f2-f52799025a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549049 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2}}{2\\sqrt{e}} & >e \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2e\\sqrt{e} \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2e\\cdot {e}^{1/2} \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2{e}^{(1+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2{e}^{3/2} \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & >3{e}^{3/2} \\\\ {e}^{x} & >{e}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3/2}) \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549050 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549050 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549050 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549050 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality ${8}^{2-4x}+11>0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549050 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1c3e141-e136-55c6-9d3b-793077b3eeeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549050 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2-4x}+11 & >0 \\\\ {8}^{2-4x} & >-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $8>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{8}^{2-4x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${8}^{2-4x}$ is greater than $-11.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{8}^{2-4x}>-11\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549065 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $5{e}^{x}+2\\le 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3a9275d-4331-54c5-bd20-117d217d8aa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549065 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{x}+2 & \\le 4 \\\\ 5{e}^{x} & \\le 2 \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\le \\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ x & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549069 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549069 #edb/ref 1069 "q-365328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549069 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549069 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x}\\ge {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549069 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9ce2daa-e38d-5bb8-b99f-f6261d8e2ed1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549069 #edb/ref 1074 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x} & \\ge {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4}] \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\ge 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8.$ Since $3<8,$ we know that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3<\\operatorname{ln}⁡8.$ Therefore, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8<0.$ Because we are dividing both sides of the inequality by a negative number, we must reverse the direction of the inequality sign:\n\n$$\nx\\le \\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
